{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Or on a distant road.</b> It is pointed (נקוד) on רחוקה, to tell you that it is not truly distant, but rather that he was standing outside the threshold of the courtyard for the entire duration of the slaughter. <br><b>The second</b> Passover: unleavened bread (matzah) and leaven (chametz) are together with it in the house, and there is no festival-day (yom tov) there, and chametz is forbidden only together with it in its eating; thus Rashi z\"l explained." "<b>And I have already</b> explained the prohibition of chametz on Passover by way of the Kabbalah, for it is so that a person not err and say that the Exodus was by the attribute of judgment (middat ha-din) alone and not by the designated Name, and this would be cutting the shoots (qotzeitz ba-neti'ot); but in the second Passover, which is outside its appointed time, it is possible for there to be matzah and chametz together with it in the house, and there is no cutting in this, for through both of them they were redeemed." "<b>And the Ramban</b> z\"l wrote: It is possible that the reason for the pointing (nequdah) is that it is distant for him in the making of the Passover — not literally distant — and therefore it went back and said ״ובדרך לא היה״ and did not mention רחוקה."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:30:59.082782475Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}